From 57a85c7e374b0f8ea40456c5b782a51304cb7093 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Fabio Campolim <250239356+fabiocampolim-design@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Fri, 28 Aug 2026 22:15:46 -0300
Subject: [PATCH 3/3] wraparound: document the meaning and periodicity of the
 momentum parameters

The docstring never says what k_x, k_y, ... are.  They are the Bloch
phases across the primitive vectors of the symmetry, so they are
2*pi-periodic and the zone is always [-pi, pi]**d in these coordinates.
Users who reparametrize them as Cartesian wave vectors (the natural thing
to do for a non-rectangular lattice, and what plot_2d_bands does for
plotting) get coordinates that are not periodic point by point; totals
such as Chern numbers survive that, local densities such as the Berry
curvature do not.  Spell this out in the Notes.
---
 src/kwant/wraparound.py | 14 ++++++++++++++
 1 file changed, 14 insertions(+)

diff --git a/src/kwant/wraparound.py b/src/kwant/wraparound.py
index 2be73bd..2263d1d 100644
--- a/src/kwant/wraparound.py
+++ b/src/kwant/wraparound.py
@@ -74,6 +74,20 @@ def wraparound(builder, keep=None, *, coordinate_names='xyz'):
 
     Notes
     -----
+    The momentum parameters are the Bloch *phases* across the primitive
+    vectors of the translational symmetry: a hopping by the symmetry element
+    ``n = (n_1, n_2, ...)`` is multiplied by ``exp(1j * (n_1 k_1 + n_2 k_2 +
+    ...))``.  Each ``k_i`` is therefore 2*pi-periodic by construction and the
+    Brillouin zone in these coordinates is always the cube ``[-pi, pi]**d``,
+    whatever the shape of the lattice.  Reparametrizing them in terms of
+    Cartesian wave vectors (multiplying by the reciprocal lattice vectors,
+    as `kwant.wraparound.plot_2d_bands` does for plotting) gives coordinates
+    that are in general *not* periodic point by point.  That is harmless for
+    quantities integrated over the whole zone, such as Chern numbers, but a
+    local quantity evaluated on such a grid, for example a Berry curvature
+    density, is wrong along the zone boundary: sample it in the native
+    ``k_i`` coordinates instead.
+
     Wraparound is stop-gap functionality until Kwant 2.x which will include
     support for higher-dimension translational symmetry in the low-level system
     format. It will be deprecated in the 2.0 release of Kwant.
-- 
